<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chiloé</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chiloé Island, Chiloé Archipelago</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chiloé is an island, the largest of an archipelago of the same name, off the coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Settled by the Spanish in 1567, it remained a royalist stronghold after mainland Chile became independent and was annexed to Chile only in January 1826. It is known for its wooden churches, several of which are listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chiloe.jpg" alt="Chiloé" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michelle Maria · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castro,_Chile_-_panoramio_(16).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Chiloé Archipelago lies in the Los Lagos Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, separated from the mainland by the 2-kilometre-wide Chacao Channel in the north, the Sea of Chiloé in the east and the Gulf of Corcovado in the southeast. Chiloé Island, at 8,394 square kilometres, is by far the largest island. Its southwestern half is a wilderness of forests, wetlands and low mountains, none higher than 800 metres, while the northeast has rolling hills of pasture and fields. The original vegetation is Valdivian temperate rainforest. The name comes from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> word, chillwe, meaning seagull place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first identifiable inhabitants are believed to be the Chonos, a seafaring people, though the Huilliche language was the main one when the Spanish arrived. Francisco de Ulloa first reached the main island in 1553, and in 1567 Captain Martín Ruiz de Gamboa conquered it with an expedition of 110 Spaniards and founded Castro. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> arrived in 1608 and built a circular mission of chapels reached by travelling in dalcas; by 1767, when the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was suppressed, there were 79 chapels.</p><p>Ancud replaced Castro as capital in 1767, and in 1784 the island became a direct dependency of the viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. A large Huilliche rebellion affected it in 1712. Chiloé never joined the Patria Vieja and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> wide support: from 1812 its men were enlisted to fight for the royalist cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and Peru. In December 1817 it became, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a>, the last stronghold of the royalists. Chilean expeditions failed at the Battle of Agüi and, in 1824, at the Battle of Mocopulli.</p><p>On 15 January 1826 Antonio de Quintanilla negotiated a surrender to a force led by Ramón Freire, and the island joined the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Charles Darwin stayed there for six months from 28 June 1834. In the 19th century expeditions to the Straits of Magellan and the colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a> set out from Chiloé, and thousands of Chilotes migrated to work on the mainland. The great earthquake of 1960 destroyed Ancud's cathedral and badly damaged Castro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The archipelago forms Chiloé Province, whose capital is Castro, with Ancud as the seat of the Catholic bishopric. Its people are called Chilotes, and its culture mixes Huilliche, Spanish and Chono influences. Wooden churches built by the Spanish and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, roofed and clad in shingles, are among its best-known buildings, and some are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. Stilt houses called palafitos stand in towns such as Castro and Chonchi. Salmon farming, fishing, tourism and agriculture are the mainstays of the economy, and Chiloé is a centre of diversity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chiloé Archipelago” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Chilean navy, commanded from 1818 by the British admiral Thomas Cochrane, then carried San Martín's Liberating Expedition to Peru in 1820, and the last royalist stronghold, the island of <b>Chiloé</b>, fell in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The heartland between the Itata River and the <b>Chiloé Archipelago</b> held the largest Indigenous population of Chile when the Spanish came: Bengoa estimates 705,000–900,000 people between the Itata and Reloncaví Sound in the mid-16th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mapuche">Mapuche · Society</a></li></ul></section>
